17592186407594 1084 #uuid "b6af969e-293e-5aa6-8efd-61f10b87dbac" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407595 1081 "images/5f/5f4468465de36289d11b86acb35c38e6d2034ca220d6559ec93b5085d7437d5c.png" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407595 1084 #uuid "e719fb82-e005-592a-9a1d-c08ce892673d" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407596 1081 "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407596 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407596 1084 #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407592 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407593 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407594 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407595 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407596 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1077 "selection" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407596 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407597 1080 #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407597 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1069 "q-122880" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1071 "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1072 #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407598 1074 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407599 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407601 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407599 1062 #uuid "32569fb7-e771-56df-bcee-088a5b89c15c" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407599 1063 #edb/ref 17592186387310 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407600 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407603 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407600 1062 #uuid "5422ee42-4513-5e58-91cc-6593fbcca408" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407600 1063 #edb/ref 17592186387276 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407601 1062 #uuid "bae2befe-109e-5175-92c1-22c16c99cc0c" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407601 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407598 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407602 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407600 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407602 1062 #uuid "e28c3322-ca5c-53ca-be1f-019e457471a4" 13194139533546 17592186045585 true] [17592186407602 1063 #e